export const FR_C2_S06_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c2-la-marge-du-cahier": [
    `I was nineteen in the spring of 1789, with fine handwriting and not a penny, which made me, in the parish of Saint-Aubin-des-Bois, in the diocese of Lisieux, the most indispensable and least regarded man in the world. Maître Lefrançois, royal notary, for whom I had been engrossing deeds for three years, had appointed me to hold the pen at the assembly of the inhabitants, which was to draw up, as in every parish in the kingdom, the book of their complaints and grievances to be carried to the king.`,
    `The assembly was held one Sunday after vespers, in the church, for want of a hall big enough. There were sixty-two of them, heads of households, ploughmen, day labourers, a miller, two weavers, the blacksmith, all in their Sunday best and all convinced that the king would read their book in person, preferably in the evening, by the fire. I did not have the heart to disabuse them. Nor am I sure, even today, that they were entirely wrong: it happens that one is read by History when one is not read by kings.`,
    `For hours they dictated to me what the parish had on its heart. The taille, which crushed the poor and spared the rich. The road corvée, which took men away in the days of the harvest. The lord's dovecote, whose pigeons devoured the sowings without anyone having the right to shoot them. The tithe, which the priest, present in the audience, defended feebly and which he admitted, to general surprise, went mostly to fatten the neighbouring abbey. I had never written so fast or so badly; they blamed me for not noting everything, and shouted all their complaints at once.`,
    `Towards the end, when tempers had calmed and the miller had stopped demanding the abolition of rival millers, the widow Leroux came forward to the table. Women had no place in the assembly, except widows who headed a household, and even they were tolerated rather than listened to. Marguerite Leroux had buried her husband at Martinmas and was raising four daughters alone on a scrap of land that would not have fed a goat. She could neither read nor write. She spoke without raising her voice.`,
    `"I'd like you to put in the book that girls should learn to read. All of them. Like the boys. So they know what they're being made to sign." There was a moment of stupor, then a burst of laughter, coarse and general, that I have never forgotten. The syndic, an important man because he owned two more cows than the others, declared that the book was meant for serious business and that one was not going to bother His Majesty with old wives' fancies. The priest said nothing. The widow Leroux went back to her seat, straight as a candle.`,
    `I was made to read the book aloud, then to have those who could sign do so, and to have those who could not make a cross. Marguerite Leroux drew hers without trembling. I watched her do it and thought that this cross, at the bottom of a text from which she had been refused, was the best proof of what she had just asked for. The book was folded, sealed, taken to the bailliage, then further on, to where history was being made, and where nobody ever asked the opinion of the parish of Saint-Aubin.`,
    `I must now make a confession that I have made to no one in forty years. When it came to making the fair copy of the book, alone in Maître Lefrançois's office, by the light of a candle that wasn't mine, I added in the margin of the last leaf, in tiny letters, with ink I had thinned so it would look older: "That girls be taught to read, like boys, so that they know what they are being made to sign." It was a forgery, strictly speaking. It was also, I believe, the only true sentence of my career as a clerk.`,
    `I am a notary in my turn today, and old. A school for girls has opened in the canton; the law required it or soon will allow it, I no longer know, laws change faster than my eyes can read. Marguerite Leroux's youngest daughter teaches there. She signs her letters in handwriting finer than mine. I never knew whether anyone, at the bailliage or at Versailles, read the margin. But I have learned, growing old, that what is written in the margins often ends up rising to the middle of the page.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-le-perruquier-de-versailles": [
    `Anselme Brodin had been dressing the court's hair for thirty years, and the court, under his hands, talked. You cannot imagine what a duchess may confide to the man who holds her head for three hours, between the hot irons and the orris powder, under a scaffolding of horsehair, gauze and feathers that makes her momentarily as still as a garden statue. Brodin had heard confessions of adultery, marriage plans, gambling debts to make a tax farmer pale, and two or three state secrets. He had never repeated a single word. It was, he said, his only nobility.`,
    `In the spring of 1787, while untangling a wig that the Marquise de Saint-Florent had had sent to him in haste, he found, rolled up in the chignon like a curl paper, a letter. He needed to read only three lines to understand that it was not from her husband, and four more to recognize, by the style even more than the signature, a prince of the blood. He refolded it with the care one takes in defusing a mine. He knew what that paper was worth, in a Paris where libels sold under the counter for a fortune and where the reputation of the great came undone faster than a curl in the rain.`,
    `Now Brodin had a daughter, Rosalie, whom he wanted to marry to an honest goldsmith in the rue Saint-Honoré, and nothing to give her as a dowry. He also had a shop whose rent kept rising, and clients who paid at Easter or Trinity, that is to say never. He kept the letter for a week in the drawer of his workbench, the way you hold an ember in your hand, wondering how long you'll be able to bear it.`,
    `It was the buyer who came to him. One evening a young man in a black coat, hat pulled down, came into the shop after closing and offered him, without preamble, two hundred louis for the marquise's letter. There was no need to explain how he knew it existed: the footmen of Versailles have ears, and the ears have rates. Two hundred louis. Rosalie's dowry, the shop saved, ten years of peace. Brodin opened the drawer. It was then that the young man took off his hat, and the wigmaker recognized his son.`,
    `He had not seen him for three years. Lucien had left one morning, slamming the door, shouting that he wouldn't spend his life powdering heads that thought only of themselves. He was said to be in London, or in prison, or with the gazette writers in Holland. In fact he wrote anonymous pamphlets against the court, which sold very well and which his father, unawares, had read with pleasure. "It's not the marquise I'm after," said Lucien. "Nor the prince. It's the world where my father has to keep quiet to eat."`,
    `The old man looked at the letter for a long time, then at his son. He thought of everything he had heard in thirty years, of all those vain and desperate heads he had held in his hands, of the duchess who wept under her wig for the son taken from her for the army, of the countess who had asked him one morning whether he believed in God, because she didn't dare ask her confessor. Lucien's world was just, perhaps. But one would not enter it, he thought, by selling other people's tears.`,
    `He held the letter to the candle. Lucien made a movement, then stopped. The paper twisted, blackened and fell in ashes on the marble of the workbench, among the pins. "If you want to overturn this world, my boy," said the wigmaker, "start by not resembling it. This world buys secrets. Yours ought to have better things to do." Lucien said nothing. He put his hat back on, then, on the threshold, turned round and asked his father whether he could come back for dinner on Sunday.`,
    `Two years later the Bastille fell. In the clubs one saw an orator named Lucien Brodin plead with remarkable eloquence for freedom of the press and, to his friends' surprise, for the privacy of correspondence. His father dressed what was left of the court until 1792, then deputies, then, it is said, a few citizenesses of the Convention, who confided their secrets to him exactly like the duchesses. He never repeated a word. Rosalie married her goldsmith. Where the dowry came from nobody ever found out; Lucien, when asked, replied that his pamphlets had sold very well.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-la-demoiselle-du-telephone": [
    `In 1900, Paris was hosting the world. The World's Fair set its moving walkways turning, lit up its Electricity Fairy, piled the pavilions of Siam, the Transvaal and Finland along the Seine, and fifty million visitors crowded in to see the coming century. Louise Marchal saw it from behind, sitting ten hours a day at a switchboard in the Gutenberg exchange, a headset on and a hundred copper plugs within reach. She was a telephone girl, and the future, for her, had the shape of a hole in a wooden panel.`,
    `The rulebook of the Post and Telegraph administration ran to forty pages and one principle: the operator does not exist. She was to utter only the prescribed formulas, "Listening," "What number do you want?", "Go ahead," "The line is busy," and never a word more. She was forbidden to listen to conversations, to respond to subscribers' jokes, to seem rushed, to seem slow, to seem anything. A supervisor, Madame Ravaut, paced the rows in her black bodice, her gaze resting on the napes of necks like a hand.`,
    `It was the night of 13 to 14 July. Louise had asked for the night shift so she could watch the parade in the morning with her sister. The exchange was almost empty; through the open windows came the children's firecrackers and the oompah of a neighbourhood ball. At one in the morning, a light came on. "Listening." A man's voice, out of breath, gave a Belleville number, a doctor's. Louise pushed in the plug. No answer. She tried again. Nothing. "The subscriber does not answer," she said, as the rules required.`,
    `Then the voice broke. The man explained, in fragments, that his wife was giving birth, that something was wrong, that the midwife had left, that he had run down to the café below to find the only telephone in the street, that he knew no other doctor. Louise looked at the night supervisor, who was dozing at the back of the room. She looked at the rulebook, open in front of her at the page of formulas. Then she pulled out the plug, pushed in another, then another, and for the first time in four years of service she spoke in her own voice.`,
    `She called the Saint-Louis hospital, which did not answer, then the local police station, where it took an eternity for someone to pick up, then, by dint of pleading with an officer, got the address of a doctor on call in the rue de Belleville. She called the café back, guided the poor man, reassured him, made him repeat the address twice. For twenty minutes, the telephone girl existed. Then the line went quiet, and Louise sat in front of her panel, hands trembling, not knowing whether she had saved someone or merely wrecked her career.`,
    `The next day, the night supervisor made her report. Louise was summoned to Madame Ravaut, who read out to her in an even voice the articles she had broken, eleven in all, and docked her three days' pay. Louise did not protest. She had not seen the parade. She had never learned how the story ended. She went back to her place, her headset, her formulas, and what she now privately called her life as the girl who doesn't exist.`,
    `A month later an envelope arrived at the Gutenberg exchange, addressed in clumsy handwriting "To the voice of 14 July." It passed from office to office, made the clerks laugh, embarrassed the head of department, who found no applicable provision in the rules, and ended up on Madame Ravaut's desk. She turned it over in her fingers for a long time. Then she crossed the room, stopped behind Louise and set the envelope down in front of her without a word.`,
    `It contained a studio photograph, slightly blurred, showing a newborn asleep in the arms of a pale, smiling woman. On the back, a single line: "Her name is Louise." When the young woman looked up, Madame Ravaut was already looking elsewhere, her back straight in her black bodice. "The rules make no provision for this kind of mail, Mademoiselle Marchal," she said without turning round. "I therefore leave you free to do with it as you wish." It was, in her whole career, the only sentence anyone ever heard her say outside the formulas.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-les-recettes-de-verdun": [
    `When Paul Granger left in August 1914, his wife Jeanne gave him one single instruction, on the platform of Périgueux station, amid the din of brass bands and farewells: "Don't lie to me. I'd rather be afraid of something than of everything." Paul promised. He did not yet know that the army had other plans for the truth, and that postal control opened letters, blacked out careless sentences and sent the most candid back to their authors with a stern note about troop morale.`,
    `Paul's first letters were therefore admirably false. He was eating well, sleeping well, morale was excellent, the Boches were retreating. Jeanne read them aloud to her mother-in-law and reread them alone at night, looking behind the words for what they did not say. She soon understood she would find nothing. Paul had always been a poor liar, and the censorship had made him a perfect one. It was she who had the idea, in the spring of 1915, in a letter that seemed to talk about nothing but cooking.`,
    `"I'm sending you your mother's recipe for confit," she wrote, "since you say you cook together with your comrades. If one day you want to tell me how things cook where you are, tell me your own recipes: I'll be sure to understand." Paul understood too. From that day on, his letters filled up with saucepans, and the censors, who had received no instructions about cooking, let through without batting an eyelid the strangest gastronomic correspondence of the war.`,
    `The code was written down nowhere; it was invented by the two of them, from one letter to the next. "Simmer for a long time over a low heat" meant waiting in the trench under slow shelling. "Sear over a high heat" announced an attack. Salt was the comrades: "I lost a lot of salt this week" was read with a heavy heart. Walnuts meant shells, because they came in clusters and you could hear them crack. And when Paul wrote "the sauce has curdled," Jeanne knew an officer had given an absurd order, that it had been obeyed, and that fewer of them had come back.`,
    `In February 1916, Paul's regiment went up to Verdun. The letters grew shorter, and the cooking more worrying. "Here we braise everything, night and day, with no lid." "No salt left at all in the soup." "The walnuts fall so thick that we no longer bother to pick them up." Jeanne learned to read these lines standing up, in the kitchen, her hand pressed against the dresser. She answered with other recipes, recipes from home, her grandmother's walnut cake, bread left to rise under a cloth, to remind him that there were still things that rise without exploding.`,
    `Then, at the beginning of May, nothing more. Three weeks without a letter. Jeanne watched for the postman, whom she had known since childhood and who had also learned to lower his eyes in front of certain doors. On 29 May, at last, there came an envelope in unfamiliar handwriting, headed with the name of a military hospital in Bar-le-Duc. Jeanne sat down before opening it. A nurse was writing, at the dictation of Private Granger, wounded in the right hand, who could no longer hold a pencil.`,
    `The letter was short, and it was a recipe. "My Jeanne, for the walnut cake, I think from now on you'll have to do the work of both hands, because I left one up there. As for the rest, the dough held. It even rose nicely. I'm coming home on convalescent leave in July; put some sugar aside, if you can find any. I never lied to you, you know. I just did some cooking."`,
    `Paul and Jeanne's great-granddaughter keeps these letters today in a tin box in Sarlat. She sometimes reads them to her pupils in the last year of collège, who laugh at first at these soldiers obsessed with confit, then stop laughing when she gives them the key. Every 11 November she makes the walnut cake. She makes it with one hand only, the left, to see, she says; and every year she finds that it is possible, but that you need someone to hold the bowl.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-le-linge-de-la-ligne": [
    `In Vierzon in 1942, the demarcation line didn't run nearby: it ran through the middle. The Cher cut the town in two, and the Occupation with it, so that you could live in the free zone and work in the occupied zone, and cross every morning, on a bridge guarded by sentries, the border between two Frances that were only one on the school maps. Élise Martineau, a laundress, crossed it twice a day, with a wheelbarrow and two big wicker baskets, to fetch the linen of the hotels on the other bank.`,
    `You get used to everything, even to showing your sheets to a foreign soldier. The sentries on the bridge changed often, but since the winter it was almost always the same one who searched her baskets, a corporal of about forty with round glasses who spoke careful schoolbook French. His name was Kurt, she had learned it by hearing a comrade call out to him; before the war he had been a primary teacher somewhere in Bavaria. He searched conscientiously, without rudeness, lifting the pillowcases with two fingers, the way you turn the pages of a pupil's exercise book.`,
    `Letters, Élise had been smuggling since the autumn. It had almost become routine: messages from separated families, sewn into the hems of tablecloths, which the official post, with its interzone cards and their pre-printed boxes, did not allow anyone to write. "In good health. Tired. Need money." You can't say your love with boxes. Élise found it right, and not very dangerous; the corporal didn't look in the hems.`,
    `At the beginning of July, the priest of Saint-Célestin called her to the sacristy. He spoke to her at length, in a low voice, about a little girl of six who had come from Paris after the round-ups of the previous month and had been hidden for ten days in an attic in the neighbourhood. Her mother had been arrested; an aunt was waiting for her on the other side, in Bourges, in the free zone. The professional smugglers asked crazy sums, and some sold out their clients. It needed someone the sentries knew and no longer distrusted. Élise asked for a night to think it over. She did not sleep.`,
    `She thought of her own son, a prisoner in Pomerania since June 1940. She thought of the penalty she would incur, which she did not know exactly, which was worse. She thought of Corporal Kurt and his two fingers on the pillowcases, and wondered what the delicacy of a man's gestures was worth when the uniform commanded something else. In the morning she went to tell the priest that she accepted. She still didn't know why; she suspected that for such things there is no reason that stands up on its own.`,
    `On 14 July, a day no longer celebrated, little Sarah curled up at the bottom of the largest basket, under a layer of damp sheets, with orders not to move, whatever happened, and to think very hard about her mother. Élise pushed the wheelbarrow onto the bridge at an ordinary pace, greeting passers-by as on any other day. The corporal was there. He lifted the first basket and put it down. Then he laid his hand on the second, and did not take it away.`,
    `Time, they say, dilates at such moments. Élise had leisure to watch a barge go by on the Cher, to notice that a button was missing from the soldier's tunic, and to understand that he had felt, beneath the linen, something warm. He looked at her over his round glasses. She held his gaze, because she had nothing else left to do. "Your laundry is very heavy today, madame," he said at last, in his textbook French. "It's the season," she replied. "The sheets don't dry well." He stood still for one more second. Then he waved her through.`,
    `Sarah reached Bourges that very evening. She lives today in Montreal, where she taught history for thirty-five years, and she came back to Vierzon once, for a plaque. Élise died in 1979 without ever knowing whether the corporal had understood, or what had become of him. She refused to be made into a heroine, and even more to have him made into a righteous man. "I don't know what he did elsewhere," she used to say. "I only know what he didn't do that day. That's already a lot, and it redeems nothing. Both are true. Make what you can of it."`,
  ],
};

